   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Personality is said with «ser»",
     "texto": [
      "To say what a person is like we use **ser**: **es amable**, **es tímida**, **son divertidos**. Before the adjective you can put **muy** or **bastante**.\n\nSome words are opposites:\n\n- **abierto** / **reservado**\n- **alegre** / **serio**\n- **generoso** / **egoísta**\n- **trabajador** / **vago**\n- **educado** / **maleducado**\n\n**Un poco** softens the idea: **es un poco serio**. But with a good quality it can sound cold: **es un poco simpático** is not clear praise."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Adjective endings and «ser» or «estar»",
     "texto": [
      "The adjective changes with the person:\n\n- **-o** becomes **-a**: serio → **seria**; generoso → **generosa**.\n- **-e** and **-ista** do not change: **alegre**, **amable**, **optimista**, **egoísta**.\n- **-dor** adds **-a**: trabajador → **trabajadora**.\n\n**Ser** says what someone is always like; **estar** says how they are now:\n\n- **Es nervioso**: he is a nervous person.\n- **Está nervioso**: today he is nervous.\n- **Es aburrido**: he bores other people.\n- **Está aburrido**: he is bored."
     ],
